.TH PODLAZ 1 "August 2026" "podlaz" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
podlaz \- CLI for Xray-compatible VPN and proxy profiles
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY podlaz
.OP --help
.YS
.SY plz
.OP --help
.YS
.SY podlaz
.B profile validate
.I profile-id
.OP --mode proxy-only|tun
.OP --json
.OP --plain
.YS
.SY podlaz
.B plan
.B --mode
.IR proxy-only | tun
.I profile-id
.OP --json
.OP --verbose
.OP --plain
.YS
.SY podlaz
.B connect
.OP --mode proxy-only|tun
.OP --handoff=block|ask|stop-known|replace-podlaz
.I profile-id
.YS
.SY podlaz
.B disconnect
.YS
.SY podlaz
.B autostart enable
.OP --mode proxy-only|tun
.I profile-id
.YS
.SY podlaz
.B autostart disable
.YS
.SY podlaz
.B autostart status
.YS
.SY podlaz
.B check
.I profile-id
.OP --target target-id
.OP --timeout duration
.OP --json
.YS
.SY podlaz
.B status
.YS
.SY podlaz
.B doctor
.RI [ options ]
.YS
.SY podlaz
.B doctor --tun
.RI [ --verbose | --json ]
.YS
.SY podlaz
.B logs
.RI [ options ]
.YS
.SY podlaz
.B recover
.OP --execute --yes
.OP --json
.YS
.SY podlaz
.B completion
.IR bash | zsh | fish
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B podlaz
manages user-owned profiles and subscriptions, renders plans and diagnostics,
and sends connection, boot-policy, or cleanup intents to the local
.BR podlazd (8)
daemon when runtime behavior is required.
.PP
Read-only commands do not require root, proxy-only mode does not mutate host
networking, and privileged TUN/recovery work belongs to daemon-owned transaction
state. Default human output is stable without ANSI color. Commands that use
symbolic status markers document
.B --plain
for ASCII-only output.
.PP
Packaged installs may provide
.B plz
as a short alias for
.BR podlaz .
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B version
Print version, commit, and build date.
.TP
.B help [command]
Print general or command-specific help.
.TP
.B completion bash|zsh|fish
Write static shell completion definitions to stdout. Generated scripts support
both
.B podlaz
and
.BR plz .
.TP
.B import <input>
Import a share URI, local profile file, or subscription URL. The command does
not connect or mutate host networking.
.TP
.B profile add --name <name> --server <host> --port <port> --protocol <protocol>
Create a manual profile.
.TP
.B profile import <share-uri>
Import one profile from a supported share URI.
.TP
.B profile list [--json]
List stored profiles.
.TP
.B profile show <profile-id> [--json]
Show one stored profile with sensitive fields redacted.
.TP
.B profile validate <profile-id> [--mode proxy-only|tun] [--json] [--plain]
Validate a profile without starting Xray, contacting the daemon, requiring root,
writing runtime config, or mutating networking. Human output is a compact
structured profile check with result, reason on failure, and next-step guidance.
.B --plain
uses ASCII status words instead of symbolic status markers.
.TP
.B profile delete <profile-id> [--yes]
Delete a profile. Non-interactive deletion requires
.BR --yes .
.TP
.B subscription add --name <name> --url <url>
Store a subscription source without fetching it.
.TP
.B subscription update <subscription-id>
Fetch a subscription and replace only profiles owned by that subscription.
.TP
.B subscription list [--json]
List subscription sources with redacted URLs.
.TP
.B subscription show <subscription-id> [--json]
Show one subscription source with redacted URL material.
.TP
.B subscription delete <subscription-id> [--yes] [--keep-profiles]
Delete subscription metadata and, by default, its owned profiles.
.TP
.B plan --mode proxy-only <profile-id> [--json]
Show a read-only proxy-only plan.
.TP
.B plan --mode tun <profile-id> [--json] [--verbose|-v] [--plain]
Show a read-only full-tunnel plan before host networking changes. Default human
output is a compact summary with profile status, the collision-free daemon-owned
TUN IPv4 address selected from the current read-only host snapshot, planned
high-level changes, blockers, warnings, safety notes, and next-step guidance.
Historical
.BR 198.18.0.1/32 ,
routing table
.BR 51820 ,
and policy priorities
.B 9999/10000
are preferred candidates only. An occupied candidate is replaced with another
verified-free session identity. Incomplete allocation evidence or exhaustion of
the bounded candidate space fails closed and renders no misleading applicable
plan. It hides raw nftables rules, rollback keys, owner labels, and long command
stderr. Use
.B --verbose
or
.B -v
for the detailed TUN/route/policy-rule/DNS/nftables/snapshot/rollback dump.
.B --plain
uses ASCII status words instead of symbolic status markers. JSON output keeps the
stable machine-readable schema and is not changed by
.BR --verbose .
.TP
.B connect [--mode proxy-only|tun] [--handoff=block|ask|stop-known|replace-podlaz] <profile-id>
Start a daemon-managed connection. The default mode is
.BR proxy-only .
For TUN mode, Xray owns
.B podlaz0
packet ingestion through its native
.B tun
inbound. Before handoff or host-networking changes, TUN mode verifies that the
packaged Xray helper accepts a minimal pinned-schema native TUN config. The
profile-generated Xray runtime config is written later after the TUN transaction
starts. Before the first host-network mutation, podlazd selects a collision-free
Network Session allocation from the authoritative host baseline and persists the
exact TUN IPv4 /32, numeric routing table, and policy priorities in transaction
desired state. After Xray creates the link, podlazd binds the exact link name,
ifindex, and TUN type, assigns the allocated address, and applies only the exact
session routes, policy rules, DNS, and nftables state. Xray remains the owner of
link creation and lifetime.
.PP
For non-interactive TUN connects, podlazd automatically recovers only exact
durable Podlaz transaction state that requires cleanup, then recollects the host
snapshot and allocates independently around unrelated host networking. Foreign
TUN devices, policy routing, route-only DNS owners, NetworkManager VPN
connections, and unrelated firewall state are baseline rather than blockers
merely because they exist. The daemon does not stop or rewrite them to make the
host look clean. Connect fails closed when recovery remains incomplete,
authoritative allocation evidence is insufficient, the bounded candidate space
is exhausted, or no concrete safe server bootstrap/data-plane plan can be built
without colliding with or mutating unowned state. If another process races into
an already allocated identity before apply, Podlaz fails instead of adopting the
foreign object as owned.
.PP
For TUN mode,
.B --handoff
selects an explicit handoff policy. The default
.B block
policy never stops a foreign VPN or removes ambiguous state; exact Podlaz
self-recovery remains enabled.
.B ask
is rejected in daemon/non-interactive connect paths and performs no recovery or
handoff mutation.
.B stop-known
is retained for CLI compatibility but does not broaden new-session authority to
stop foreign NetworkManager VPN connections; collision-aware allocation treats
them as baseline.
.B replace-podlaz
may disconnect the exact active Podlaz TUN session before starting a new
allocation. Unsupported handoff values fail before network mutation.
.PP
If TUN
.B network-apply,
.B network-verify,
or later connectivity verification fails, podlazd runs bounded redacted
diagnostics while the failed applied state still exists and stores the latest
report before the first rollback command. The report includes a stable primary
classification,
.B failure_phase,
and
.B rollback_status;
known network lifecycle failures use
.B network_apply_failure
or
.BR network_verify_failure .
Rollback atomically finalizes the historical status as
.B completed
or
.BR failed .
Static resolved ownership is not sufficient for commit. The daemon also
requires the exact allocated TUN address, an uncached IPv4 query bound to
.BR podlaz0 ,
a separate normal system resolver lookup, and a TUN route for at least one
returned IPv4 address.
.B Current Scopes
is diagnostic evidence only. Diagnostic failure does not suppress cleanup. The
returned error includes the safe classification and report path when available
and directs the user to
.BR "podlaz doctor --tun --verbose" .
.PP
On success, normal human output is intentionally concise: the first line is
.B Connected,
followed by the redacted profile display name and selected mode. Detailed routes,
DNS, firewall, runtime configuration, and transaction identifiers remain in
operator diagnostics rather than the default lifecycle output.
.TP
.B disconnect
Stop the active daemon-managed connection. Safe to repeat. Successful human
output is one
.B Disconnected
line.
.TP
.B autostart enable [--mode proxy-only|tun] <profile-id>
Enable explicit boot autostart for a future boot. The CLI reads and validates the
user-owned profile through the same profile validation boundary as normal
connect, then submits only the minimal canonical connection snapshot to the
daemon. The command does not connect immediately. Policy written in the current
boot is deliberately ineligible for an automatic connect caused only by a
same-boot daemon restart.
.TP
.B autostart disable
Remove future-boot autostart policy. This does not disconnect an active session,
cancel an admitted current-boot attempt, or reset the consumed one-attempt/no-retry
authority for the current boot.
.TP
.B autostart status
Read boot-autostart policy without mutating networking. Disabled output is
.BR "Autostart: Disabled" .
Enabled output begins with
.BR "Autostart: Enabled for next boot" ,
and may also show the redacted profile display name and mode. Autostart JSON is
not a public schema yet.
.PP
At daemon startup, an existing current-boot Network Session continuation,
recovery, or terminal-cleanup authority always has priority over fresh autostart.
Only when no competing Network Session remains may one logical autostart attempt
be admitted for the current Linux boot. The admitted attempt pins the exact
request, waits for bounded fresh uplink-readiness evidence, and enters the same
canonical connection lifecycle as an explicit connect. Daemon replacement may
continue an
.B in_progress
attempt; a
.B succeeded
or conclusively
.B terminal
attempt prevents a second automatic connect until the next boot. Explicit
disconnect, package replacement, or a later runtime terminal failure does not
reset that authority.
.TP
.B check <profile-id> [--target <target-id>] [--timeout <duration>] [--json]
Run explicit bounded proxy-only connectivity diagnostics for one profile.
.TP
.B check --all [--target <target-id>] [--timeout <duration>] [--json]
Check all stored profiles with deterministic output and a small default
concurrency limit.
.TP
.B status
Print the concise product lifecycle view. Daemon-backed detailed evidence remains
available to doctor/recover and the daemon status model, but normal human status
renders one of
.BR "Status: Connected" ,
.BR "Status: Connecting" ,
.BR "Status: Reconnecting" ,
.BR "Status: Disconnected" ,
or
.BR "Status: Unknown" .
.PP
.B Disconnected
means the lifecycle is conclusively inactive. A stopped/inaccessible daemon,
incomplete local inspection, stale/ambiguous evidence, or another state that
does not prove inactivity is
.B Unknown
rather than a false disconnected claim.
.B Connecting
is an admitted connect that has not yet established the product session.
.B Reconnecting
is an established protected session while current evidence is being
revalidated/rebuilt. These transient lifecycle phases are not diagnostic failure
merely because they are in progress.
.PP
For an active TUN session, durable transaction state and current network health
are separate facts. A committed transaction may be published temporarily as
.BR revalidating
or
.BR degraded
while fresh generation-specific evidence is being established or a terminal
verification outcome is being handed off. These are not stable end states after
a proved verification failure or revalidation deadline.
.PP
The daemon publishes a positive
.B network_generation
and a stable classification while current health is not verified. Generation 1
becomes
.B verified
only after the exact post-commit authoritative observation has passed the same
canonical state and layered connectivity verification used for later
revalidation.
.PP
If a fresh owned session fails required verification or reaches the bounded
revalidation deadline, podlazd keeps the old health proof invalid, records a
bounded redacted TUN diagnostic report before cleanup, releases revalidation
authority, and automatically invokes the normal bounded lifecycle disconnect.
Successful rollback converges to inactive state, so active
.B tun_health
is no longer published. Failed rollback keeps the surviving session or owned
state fail-closed as
.B cleanup-required
for explicit recovery. Cancellation caused by an explicit user disconnect,
recovery, or daemon shutdown is not treated as a second terminal failure and
does not schedule another automatic disconnect.
.PP
A conclusively terminal lifecycle may add one short stable
.B Reason:
line while the terminal outcome remains the latest relevant admitted lifecycle.
The reason is typed product state, not parsed diagnostic/error prose. A newer
explicit request rejected before lifecycle admission leaves the previous valid
reason unchanged. A newer admitted lifecycle supersedes it; if that new explicit
connect itself reaches conclusively clean terminal failure, it records its own
new connect-failure reason. The persistent boot-attempt record remains separate
because it exists only to forbid a second automatic connect in the same boot.
.TP
.B doctor [--json]
Print environment and runtime diagnostics. Daemon-backed diagnostics use typed
lifecycle and transaction ownership to distinguish exact resources expected for
a clean active TUN session from stale state. Missing resources, link identity
mismatch, incomplete transaction authority, cleanup-required state, and ambiguous
ownership remain warnings. Local fallback remains conservative and does not infer
ownership without daemon authority. Diagnostics are read-only.
.TP
.B doctor --tun [--verbose|-v|--json]
Run daemon-backed, read-only diagnostics for the active TUN session or return the
saved latest TUN report. The bounded dependency-aware sequence checks session and
ownership metadata, the VPN server bypass, IPv4 policy routing, systemd-resolved
link ownership, DNS over UDP and TCP, positive system resolution, reserved
.invalid NXDOMAIN integrity, TCP/443, TLS, small HTTPS, two independent DoH
providers, IPv6 state, and guarded PMTU evidence. Compact output reports the
primary classification and next step. Verbose output adds bounded evidence.
Historical failed-connect reports expose
.B failure_phase
and
.BR rollback_status .
JSON uses the same centrally redacted schema with
.BR schema_version=1 .
The command does not change routes, policy rules, DNS, MTU, nftables, services,
other VPNs, or browser state.
.TP
.B doctor --core --xray <path> [--json]
Validate an explicitly supplied Xray-compatible core binary.
.TP
.B logs [--follow|-f] [--daemon] [--core] [--since <duration>]
Read recent podlaz journal entries. The
.B --since
value is one positive decimal integer followed by exactly one of
.BR s ,
.BR m ,
or
.BR h ,
for example
.BR 30s ,
.BR 15m ,
.BR 2h ,
or
.BR 36h ,
up to
.BR 720h .
Zero, signed, fractional, compound, unsupported-unit, date-like, and native
journalctl expressions are rejected as invalid usage before journalctl starts.
Valid values are translated to one relative journal argument, for example
.BR "--since -36h" .
The same contract applies to daemon, core, and follow modes.
.TP
.B recover
Inspect stale podlaz-owned recovery candidates without changing the host.
.TP
.B recover --execute --yes [--json]
Ask the daemon to clean clearly podlaz-owned stale state. For the validated
podlaz-owned
.B podlaz0
target, only normal
.B resolvectl
exit status 1 with empty stdout and the exact bounded supported
.B No such device
stderr is accepted as idempotent cleanup. This includes the exact Ubuntu 24.04
form
.BR "Failed to resolve interface \"podlaz0\", ignoring: No such device" .
Cancellation, deadline expiry, process launch failure, signal termination,
permission denial, other exit codes, extra output, unrelated exit status 1, or
different/unbounded output remains a failure. A fresh successful daemon scan is
authoritative; a failed scan reports incomplete status rather than reusing older
candidates.
Cleanup-complete terminal transaction history is non-blocking; recovery and an
immediate subsequent TUN connect must not observe a cleanup-required phantom
transaction or stale scan candidate.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --json
Print stable JSON for commands whose JSON contract is implemented. Autostart JSON
remains deferred.
.TP
.B --verbose, -v
For
.B plan --mode tun,
print detailed technical planning sections instead of the compact default human
summary. For
.BR doctor --tun ,
print bounded route, DNS, TLS, HTTP, IPv6, command, and timing evidence.
.TP
.B --plain
For commands that use symbolic human status markers, print ASCII status words
such as OK, WARN, BLOCKED, and SKIP.
.TP
.B --yes
Confirm destructive state removal or recovery execution.
.TP
.B --mode proxy-only|tun
Select connection, autostart, planning, or validation mode.
.TP
.B --handoff=block|ask|stop-known|replace-podlaz
For TUN connects, select the explicit handoff policy. Unsupported handoff values
are rejected before network mutation. Boot autostart does not persist a handoff
policy.
.TP
.B --target <target-id>
Select a service target for
.BR check .
.TP
.B --timeout <duration>
Set the per-probe timeout for
.BR check .
.TP
.B --follow, -f
Follow journal output for
.BR logs .
.TP
.B --daemon
Select daemon log lines for
.BR logs .
.TP
.B --core
Select core diagnostics or core log lines, depending on the command.
.TP
.B --tun
Select layered daemon-backed TUN diagnostics for
.BR doctor .
.TP
.B --xray <path>
Provide the core binary path for
.BR "doctor --core" .
.TP
.B --since <duration>
For
.BR logs ,
accept exactly one positive decimal integer plus
.BR s ,
.BR m ,
or
.BR h ,
with a maximum of
.BR 720h .
Invalid values return exit status 2 before journalctl is started.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success. A normal
.B Connecting
or
.B Reconnecting
product lifecycle phase is not unhealthy merely because it is in progress.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime or operation failure.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage, invalid flags or arguments, or deferred JSON output.
.TP
.B 3
Diagnostic command found confirmed unhealthy or unavailable evidence, including
active TUN health that is genuinely degraded/cleanup-required. A lifecycle phase
alone is not sufficient for exit status 3.
.TP
.B 4
Permission or authorization failure.
.TP
.B 5
Required daemon access was unavailable.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/podlaz
User configuration and profiles.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/podlaz
User state.
.TP
.I /var/lib/podlaz/boot-autostart-manifest.json
Daemon-private persistent Boot Autostart Manifest under systemd
.BR StateDirectory=podlaz .
It is mode 0600 and contains only the minimal validated future-boot connection
snapshot and boot/generation authority.
.TP
.I /run/podlaz
Daemon runtime directory, socket, generated config metadata, and transaction
state.
.TP
.I /run/podlaz/boot-autostart-attempt.json
Current-boot private pinned autostart attempt and one-attempt/no-retry authority.
.TP
.I /run/podlaz/product-terminal-reason.json
Current-boot private typed product terminal outcome/supersede state. It is not
autostart retry authority.
.TP
.I /run/podlaz/diagnostics/tun-last.json
Daemon-owned, atomically replaced latest TUN diagnostic report. The report is
centrally redacted, bounded to 256 KiB, and stored with mode 0600.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR podlazd (8)
